            <ArticleTitle>ASICs Mediate Pain and Inflammation in Musculoskeletal Diseases.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>449-59</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1152/physiol.00030.2015</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Chronic musculoskeletal pain is debilitating and affects ∼ 20% of adults. Tissue acidosis is present in painful musculoskeletal diseases like rheumatoid arthritis. ASICs are located on skeletal muscle and joint nociceptors as well as on nonneuronal cells in the muscles and joints, where they mediate nociception. This review discusses the properties of different types of ASICs, factors affecting their pH sensitivity, and their role in musculoskeletal hyperalgesia and inflammation.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>©2015 Int. Union Physiol. Sci./Am. Physiol. Soc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
